TOP FIVE: MY FAVORITE QUESTS
by Chendailem

There are many great things to see and explore in NannyMUD. The complex and
  fascinating areas, the interesting creatures living everywhere, the clubs,
  the guilds, the weapons, the different ways to die, the lag, the spam, the
  mudsex, the puzzles, the extraordinary fact that alcohol is *good* for your
  body and, of course, the amazing social life in here and the people who stand
  behind all the letters and lines flying past the telnet client forming
  friendships for life. Let's just agree right now; this is one heck of an
  amazing place to hang out.

In this article I want to put the spotlight on one of the things I really like
  about NannyMUD -- the quests -- and give you a small toplist of the five
  quests I like the most. Quests are wonderful things. When you get tired of
  running around killing bunnies all day, you can pick a quest, and suddenly,
  your gameplay has a *purpose*. Sure, it may be many different ways to gain
  that purpose, and you can always pick another quest if you want another
  purpose, but that's perhaps what I like the most about them. They're
  non-linear. You don't have to start at "Bells of St Mary" and solve every
  quest up until "Inspire Zeus and Discover Revenge" in that order. You pick
  the ones you like, and solve them the way you want to solve them.

Besides, the diversity of the quests makes them suit almost everybody. Do you
  want a quest that involves no killing, or a quest that involves tons of
  killing? Do you want a quest that will probably shift your alignment towards
  saintly, or a quest that will shift your alignment towards demonic? Do you
  want a quest that gives you a huge reward and tons of experience points, or a
  quest you can solve while waiting for the potato water to boil? Well, take a
  pick, it's most likely in your Book of Quests.

Personally, I'm not that much of a killer, so if a quest description goes "You
  need to kill fifty dragons, each of them strong enough to destroy you with
  one hit, the healing will cost you millions of gold coins and there's a 93 %
  chance that you die", I tend to skip that one. Basically, that means that my
  personal top five quest list may consist of quests you will hate. Still, that
  doesn't mean I wouldn't like reading *your* top lists. I'm always curious to
  find out what quests other players prefer, and -- most important -- why.

"Enough with the yabbing," you probably think, "I came here to read a list! I
  want to see scores and points and numbers and preferrably a bottom list so
  that I can laugh at all the wizards making terrible quests!" Calm down a
  little, or I won't give you a list at all. This isn't meant to make fun of
  anybody, nor is it meant to rate anything. This is just a list of five
  recommendations, with descriptions of how I like them. Got that? Okay. Good.
  Then...

... on to the list.



Quests that *almost* made it into the list: "Four in a row" (Banshee), "Help
  Sir Guillotine build his machine" (Gurk) and "Help Lorn prepare for Pogo's
  visit" (Leowon). I recommend reading up on them if you're more interested,
  even though I won't go into further detail here doesn't mean they're not
  great quests.



NUMBER FIVE: "Year of Banshee" (220 points, by Banshee)

Are you a newbie? In that case, SOLVE "YEAR OF BANSHEE". I remember back when I
  was new to the game. Reading the astonishing list of all the quests available
  in the game, I remember thinking "Wow, I'd better start at the bottom of
  this; buy a Book of Quests, visit the tourist information office, and MAYBE
  slay those orcs and get that sword when I'm strong enough". Basically, I
  stayed at the low-point quests because I was scared to try those more
  difficult quests. I was just a newbie, there was no way I could slay lag or
  kill ten different dragons, right? Right, but there are *some* high-score
  quests that don't require much killing and that can be solved by most
  players. "Year of Banshee" is not only possible to solve for a newbie, it
  seems to be *created* for newbies, but at 220 points, I would probably never
  have solved it as a newbie if I hadn't happened to stumble over it by
  accident (or maybe someone recommended it, I can't remember at the moment).

The concept is easy. You need a vacation from the world of Nanny, so you go to
  the old crone in Banshee's area and ask her for a holiday. She transfer you
  to another world, where you must solve twelve different puzzles, one for each
  month of the year. What's required to solve these puzzles? Well,
  understanding how to use the commands of Nanny, and... that's it, really. If
  you came to Nanny because you want to grow in strength and kill as many
  trolls as you can, you probably won't like Year of Banshee much -- I think
  the quest has a total of *one* small kill. However, doing this quest at an
  early stage of the game is very clever, because that way *you learn more
  about how the game works*. More precisely, you learn to watch your
  surroundings carefully, you learn to guess which command to use next, and you
  learn how to communicate with NannyMUD. Strongly recommended for all new
  players (though, you need to save up a certain amount of experience points
  before the crone will find that you need a holiday -- but save this one as
  soon as you can!).



NUMBER FOUR: "Rescue the Picnickers" (45 points, by Bixby)

Bixby's quests are known for revolving around logic and mathematics, and this
  one is no exception. I mention this quest in special because I somehow get
  the feeling that it's not as well known of as Bixby's other quests. Most
  people have played "Help the Apothecary", and the more daring ones take the
  step onto "Bring Out the Diadem", but this one I'm not sure if everybody even
  knows about. If you like logics and mathematical puzzles, you will like this
  one.

Three couples are stranded on an island. You have to save them. Them being
  extremely jealous and not being able to trust each other doesn't make it any
  easier. Yeah, it's a boat puzzle. Bring them to the other side using as few
  moves as possible. If you manage to keep a straight head, you shouldn't have
  much problems scoring the quest points for this one.



NUMBER THREE: "Build a new guitar for the old musician" (48 points, by Neill)

For some reason, this is always one of the first quests I play when I start up
  with a new player. It can be solved relatively easily (especially the second
  time around), and again, no killing is involved. But there's somehow an
  unexplainable cozy feeling to the quest that I like.

You have to enter the castle of Neill (by answering a music-related question)
  and build a guitar from parts you find all around the castle. There's also a
  manual hidden somewhere in the castle. A pretty easy one, but that's some of
  the charm, too. Also, in my opinion, this is one of the easiest ways to get a
  guitar in the game, and when you have a guitar you can join the Guitarists
  club. I love that club.

What did you say? You think you're sensing a pattern, that these are only
  quests that take part in isolated parts of the MUD who require little killing
  and no socialization with the other players whatsoever? Well, you may be
  right, maybe I like that kind of quests. But don't worry, up next is a quest
  that demolishes that pattern...



NUMBER TWO: "Help the Servant" (60 points, by Brom)

If you've been playing NannyMUD for a while and you still haven't tried this
  one, you're seriously missing out. This is one of the most important quests
  of the game, as it requires you to be a decent player. To solve the quest,
  you must have enough Nanny knowledge to know your way around, you must have
  enough strength to kill a bunch of animals, and you must in most cases
  have enough social skills to get another player to help you.

Brom's servant has been dwarved, and you need to cook up a potion to help him
  get back to his own self again. The warlock will give you a basic potion, but
  some ingredients are missing, and you need to find them. Most ingredients
  come from different animals, so you need to have some skill in figuring out
  where the different kinds of animals can be living. Most ingredients can only
  be taken from the animal if it's dead, so you have to be strong enough to
  kill it. And sometimes it is required that the animal is killed by, or
  fighting, someone other than you. Challenging? Nah, it's pretty simple,
  really, as long as you master a few of the most vital skills in the game.



NUMBER ONE: "The Asylum quest" (120 points, by Czygorwan)

This quest has everything. Some killing is involved, so you shouldn't be a
  total wimp, but the job description states "Muscles not needed", so you
  won't need to be Tarzan, either. A lot of thinking is involved, so you
  shouldn't be a total jackass. The quest has complexity, many different parts,
  you have to figure a lot out to solve it, and when you finally manage to
  solve it, it really feels good.

You are a stand-in doctor at the Five Finger Asylum, and have to find a way to
  cure the patients there. That's right, patients in plural. You have... five
  or six different patients, and each of them has completely different
  illnesses (though, all mental). Cure them all, and the quest points are yours.

It should be noted that the quest has a... I'm not sure if it's a bug or a
  feature, but at one point in the quest you need two identical items. I have
  never been able to find more than one of them. As there's a good chance this
  *isn't* a bug, I won't reveal here how I solved that problem, but I *will*
  wish you good luck in the quest.



These are my favorite quests. There may be some of them you hate. There may be
  some quests you like better, and there's even a chance that your favorites
  are quests I've never even discovered. If that's the case, I would love
  hearing about them -- but any way, there's no way a top five list of any kind
  won't be subjective. Different kinds of players like different kinds of
  quests.

Anyhow: Questing is no longer required for advancing levels, so if you don't
  like questing at all, you can easily skip that part. But should you get tired
  of killing bunnies, do yourself a favor and pick up your Book of Quests every
  now and then. And should you choose to solve the quests I recommend above,
  these five quests alone will easily give you almost five hundred quest points.

Good luck!
